def before(p,text='',style=None,align=None,bold=False):
 q=OxmlElement('w:p');p._p.addprevious(q)
 from docx.text.paragraph import Paragraph
 par=Paragraph(q,p._parent)
 if style:par.style=(style.replace(' ','') if style and style.startswith('Heading ') else style)
 if text:
  r=par.add_run(text);r.bold=bold;r.font.name='Times New Roman';r.font.size=Pt(12);r._element.rPr.rFonts.set(qn('w:eastAsia'),'Times New Roman')
 par.paragraph_format.line_spacing=2;par.paragraph_format.space_after=Pt(0)
 if align is not None:par.alignment=align
 elif style not in ['Heading 1','Heading 2']:par.alignment=WD_ALIGN_PARAGRAPH.JUSTIFY
 return par
pre=[('CHAPTER 1: INTRODUCTION','Heading 1'),('1.1 Background','Heading 2'),('Infrastructure as Code (IaC) has changed how organisations provision and maintain cloud environments. Instead of configuring each resource through a provider console, teams define infrastructure in files that can be reviewed, versioned, tested, and applied repeatedly. This approach supports consistency and speed, but it also makes configuration errors repeatable. A permissive security group, unencrypted storage setting, or over-broad identity policy can be copied across environments just as easily as a secure configuration. Empirical work has documented security concerns in IaC scripts and the need for systematic quality controls (Rahman et al., 2023; Verdet et al., 2024).','Normal'),('Terraform is a prominent IaC platform because its HashiCorp Configuration Language describes resources declaratively and works across multiple cloud providers (HashiCorp, 2024; Morris, 2021). Teams can represent networks, compute, storage, identity, and managed services in a shared codebase. This allows security checks to run during development, before resources are created. It also means that a mistake in a reusable module can affect multiple deployments. AWS provides a useful bounded setting for research because Terraform configurations commonly define services such as Amazon S3, EC2, RDS, IAM, EKS, and KMS, each with security-sensitive options.','Normal'),('IaC changes the timing and scale of security review. Infrastructure can be examined before deployment, while a configuration is still being edited. Static analysis tools such as Checkov and Trivy support this shift by applying security rules to source files. They can identify known unsafe settings consistently and at low marginal cost. Their results, however, depend on the rules implemented in their policy catalogues and on how each tool represents a configuration. Different scanners can describe the same risk with distinct policy identifiers or divide a broad security property into multiple findings.','Normal'),('Large language models (LLMs) offer a possible complementary form of review. They can explain code in natural language and consider relationships among blocks, variables, and references. These strengths may help identify risks that depend on context, but fluent output is not evidence of accuracy. Models can omit real issues, raise unsupported concerns, and provide fixes that are too general or technically incorrect. Recent work has examined LLMs for cloud threat modelling and Terraform code smells, but those tasks do not directly establish detection accuracy for Terraform security misconfigurations (Munshi et al., 2025; Vo et al., 2025).','Normal'),('1.2 Problem Statement','Heading 2'),('Cloud environments contain interconnected resources, and security weaknesses may emerge from the way their settings combine. A database may be encrypted but publicly reachable. A compute instance may use a permissive role while receiving sensitive values through user data. A storage bucket may have a private access control list but lack a complete public-access block. Reviewers therefore need to determine not only whether a suspicious string exists, but whether the configuration creates a meaningful security risk in context.','Normal'),('Rule-based scanners provide repeatable checks for known patterns, yet they can only report conditions encoded in their policies. They may not explain interactions across multiple resources, and their policy coverage differs. A direct comparison of raw counts can consequently mislead. A valid comparison needs a defined unit of analysis, a defensible mapping between tool findings, and clear treatment of issues found by only one tool.','Normal'),('An LLM could potentially explain relationships among Terraform blocks and recommend a concrete correction. It could also miss a misconfiguration, infer a problem that is not present, or recommend an incomplete fix. Without labelled findings and explicit evaluation measures, a convincing response cannot be treated as detection accuracy. This study addresses the need for a controlled comparison using precision, recall, F1-score, category-level analysis, and a rubric for remediation quality.','Normal'),('1.3 Research Aim and Questions','Heading 2'),('The aim is to evaluate how accurately selected LLMs identify security misconfigurations in AWS Terraform configuration compared with static analysis tools, and to assess the quality of the models’ remediation recommendations. The study is guided by three questions.','Normal'),('RQ1: What level of accuracy can LLMs achieve in detecting security misconfigurations in Terraform configurations, compared with Checkov and Trivy?','Normal'),('RQ2: Which categories of Terraform security misconfigurations are most and least effectively identified by LLMs?','Normal'),('RQ3: To what extent do LLM-generated remediation recommendations align with expert-defined fixes for identified Terraform security misconfigurations?','Normal'),('1.4 Scope','Heading 2'),('The empirical scope is Terraform HCL for AWS resources. TerraGoat provides a controlled initial dataset of 14 AWS Terraform files. The methodology also plans a wider GitHub corpus and Terraform Registry modules, but those collections are not present in the current analysis. The proposed LLM set is GPT-4o, Claude Sonnet 4.6, and Gemini 2.5 Pro, each under zero-shot and few-shot prompting. No external model API evaluation has been conducted for this draft. The temporal boundary in the original proposal is mid-2026; this document records project status as of October 2026.','Normal'),('The study evaluates source-level configuration findings. It does not establish whether a deployed AWS account is exploitable, nor does it measure runtime detection or the operational cost of remediating a live environment. Checkov and Trivy results are treated as tool outputs rather than infallible expert truth. The current comparison is limited to TerraGoat and a manually constructed semantic policy crosswalk that still needs independent review.','Normal'),('1.5 Dissertation Structure','Heading 2'),('Chapter 2 reviews IaC security, static analysis, LLM-based code analysis, prompt design, and the research gap. Chapter 3 describes the research design, datasets, ground-truth construction, prompts, metrics, and threats to validity. Chapter 4 reports preliminary Checkov results for TerraGoat AWS. Chapter 5 updates the comparison with Trivy and discusses the limits of the current evidence. Chapter 6 summarises what can and cannot yet be concluded and identifies the work required to complete or reframe the model evaluation.','Normal')]

