chapter5=[('CHAPTER 5: RESULTS AND DISCUSSION','Heading 1'),('5.1 Introduction','Heading 2'),('This chapter reports the completed static analysis of the TerraGoat AWS module and clarifies the status of the planned LLM evaluation. Checkov and Trivy outputs are available for the 14-file Terraform module. Their policy namespaces are not numerically interchangeable, so the original notebook’s direct numeric conversion of Trivy AWS identifiers was not suitable for determining agreement. A manual semantic crosswalk was prepared using the control descriptions and same-resource Checkov findings. The crosswalk provides a provisional comparison dataset, not a final expert-validated ground truth. No LLM responses were generated for this draft. RQ1, RQ2, and RQ3 therefore remain unanswered empirically.','Normal'),('5.2 Ground Truth Summary','Heading 2'),('Checkov reported 221 failed checks across its scanned result types. Restricting the analysis to Terraform checks gives 215 findings. The six additional records comprise two Docker checks and four secret-scanner checks, so they are excluded from the Terraform-only comparison. Trivy 0.73.0 reported 113 failed Terraform misconfiguration records in the same AWS directory; two additional findings in its report concern a Dockerfile and are outside this comparison. These raw counts are not directly comparable because the tools use different policy catalogues and may split or combine controls differently.','Normal'),('The Trivy report uses identifiers in the AWS-00xx format. These are Aqua policy identifiers, not Checkov identifiers with zero-padded numbers. The crosswalk therefore matches the documented security control rather than equating the numeric suffix. A Trivy finding was retained as consensus only where a corresponding Checkov policy failed on the same Terraform resource in the same file. Repeated tool records mapping to the same resource and Checkov policy were grouped to avoid counting one control multiple times. The Terraform-scoped data contain 95 unique Checkov policy IDs and 47 unique Trivy policy IDs. The analyst-created crosswalk links 41 Trivy policy IDs to candidate Checkov equivalents. It yields 75 candidate consensus groups, 140 Checkov-only groups, and 8 Trivy-only groups. Six Trivy policy IDs have no direct semantic mapping in the crosswalk. The detailed mapping is supplied in Appendix C and the working CSV files.','Normal'),('Table 5.1. Static analysis findings for TerraGoat AWS','Normal'),('','TABLE1'),('The 75 candidate consensus groups are concentrated in encryption at rest (20), network exposure (12), availability and resilience (10), storage configuration (9), public storage exposure (8), other controls (8), logging and monitoring (5), hardcoded secrets (2), and IAM over-permissioning (1). These figures describe the composition of the analyst-adjudicated consensus candidate set. They are not model detection scores and should not be interpreted as prevalence estimates for production Terraform.','Normal'),('Table 5.2. Candidate consensus groups by category','Normal'),('','TABLE2'),('The distribution by file is also uneven. The largest candidate group is rds.tf with 18 resource-policy matches. ec2.tf and s3.tf each contain 14, followed by db-app.tf with 11 and eks.tf with 7. The remaining matches occur in ecr.tf, es.tf, neptune.tf, kms.tf, and lambda.tf. Four files have no candidate consensus groups under this crosswalk. These differences reflect both the deliberately vulnerable contents of TerraGoat and the coverage of the two scanning tools.','Normal'),('Table 5.3 is descriptive of this single benchmark. In rds.tf, the aligned controls include encryption, backup retention, deletion protection, and authentication. In ec2.tf, the candidate matches include EBS encryption, metadata service tokens, exposed network rules, and subnet address assignment. In s3.tf, the matches include public-access controls, logging, versioning, and encryption. These examples show why policy meaning and resource identity both matter when reconciling the tools.','Normal'),('Table 5.3. Candidate consensus groups by Terraform file','Normal'),('','TABLE3'),('Figure 5.1 compares raw tool finding totals with the deduplicated semantic consensus groups. The denominator differs by series: Checkov and Trivy show raw Terraform findings, while consensus shows grouped resource-policy matches. Figure 5.2 shows the distribution of the 75 candidate groups by the category assigned from the mapped control.','Normal'),('5.3 RQ1: Overall Detection Accuracy','Heading 2'),('The current data do not support a numerical answer to RQ1. There are no raw responses from GPT-4o, Claude Sonnet 4.6, or Gemini 2.5 Pro in either prompt condition. Without model outputs, true positives, false positives, and false negatives cannot be counted. Precision, recall, and F1-score are consequently not calculated. The Checkov and Trivy totals are useful baselines, but they cannot substitute for measurements of model performance.','Normal'),('5.4 RQ2: Category-Level Analysis','Heading 2'),('The static findings show that the candidate consensus set includes multiple categories, with encryption at rest and network exposure most frequent in this TerraGoat module. Figure 5.2 reports these counts. It does not show which categories an LLM identifies effectively. That question requires comparing model detections with the same adjudicated ground truth within each category. The sample is also a deliberately vulnerable demonstration repository, so the category distribution reflects its design and cannot be generalised to ordinary repositories.','Normal'),('5.5 RQ3: Remediation Quality','Heading 2'),('Remediation quality has not been scored because no LLM recommendations are available. The planned three-point rubric remains appropriate for later evaluation: a score of 2 indicates a correct and specific fix, 1 indicates a partially correct fix, and 0 indicates an incorrect or absent fix. The Trivy resolution text and Checkov policy descriptions can help define expected remediations, but they are not substitutes for assessing model-generated advice.','Normal'),('5.6 Discussion','Heading 2'),('The main result from this phase is methodological. Tool agreement cannot be estimated by converting a Trivy AWS identifier such as AWS-0028 to CKV_AWS_28 solely on the basis of the matching number. The two catalogues encode controls differently. A resource-level semantic mapping is therefore needed before a consensus benchmark can be constructed. The current crosswalk makes that mapping explicit and reproducible, while retaining unmatched findings as tool-only. A second reviewer should inspect the mapping, particularly where a broad policy corresponds to several narrower controls.','Normal'),('The study’s comparison is related to, but distinct from, ACSE-Eval, which evaluates cloud threat identification and threat modelling across AWS deployment scenarios (Munshi et al., 2025). The present work focuses on Terraform HCL misconfiguration detection at resource-block level and proposes precision, recall, and F1-score. Vo et al. (2025) study Terraform code-smell detection, a related code-analysis task that does not establish security-finding accuracy for the present dataset. Since this draft contains no LLM outputs, it cannot claim that any model exceeds or trails these studies or the static tools.','Normal'),('5.7 Limitations','Heading 2'),('Four threats described in Chapter 3 remain material. First, a two-tool consensus can omit issues missed by both scanners. Second, manual policy mapping introduces subjectivity; documenting the crosswalk improves traceability but does not remove this threat, and independent review is outstanding. Third, model outputs can vary across runs; no model runs are available to assess that variation. Fourth, TerraGoat is one intentionally vulnerable AWS repository, so results from this module alone do not establish performance on production repositories or other cloud providers. The wider GitHub and Terraform Registry datasets have not been collected.','Normal')]
