ch6=[('CHAPTER 6: CONCLUSION','Heading 1'),('6.1 Summary of Findings','Heading 2'),('RQ1 asks how accurately LLMs detect Terraform misconfigurations compared with static tools. It remains unanswered because this draft contains no model responses, so precision, recall, and F1-score cannot be calculated. The available static baseline contains 215 Checkov Terraform findings and 113 Trivy Terraform findings, with 75 provisional semantic consensus groups after analyst crosswalk and resource-level deduplication.','Normal'),('RQ2 asks which misconfiguration categories LLMs identify most and least effectively. It remains unanswered because there are no category-labelled model predictions. The current consensus candidate contains varied controls, particularly encryption, networking, availability, and storage, but that distribution describes the test module rather than LLM performance.','Normal'),('RQ3 asks whether LLM remediation recommendations align with expert-defined fixes. It remains unanswered because no LLM recommendations were collected or scored. The three-point rubric is specified, but applying it requires model-generated remediation text and independent scoring criteria.','Normal'),('6.2 Contributions','Heading 2'),('The completed work establishes a controlled research question, a resource-level evaluation design, a reproducible TerraGoat AWS dataset, and preliminary Checkov and Trivy scan results. It also exposes an important comparison issue: policy identifiers from distinct scanners cannot be equated by numeric suffix alone. The manual crosswalk records candidate semantic equivalences and separates them from unmatched tool findings. This provides a transparent starting point for expert adjudication and later model evaluation.','Normal'),('The current results are preliminary rather than a completed test of LLM effectiveness. Their contribution is chiefly procedural: they identify how to compare heterogeneous scanner outputs without claiming that raw count differences represent accuracy.','Normal'),('A further contribution is the distinction between raw finding records and deduplicated resource-policy groups. Without this distinction, repeated nested controls can make one tool appear to produce more independent issues than another. The exported comparison records retain the original IDs, names, affected resources, and match status so that later reviewers can reproduce or challenge the matching decisions.','Normal'),('6.3 Limitations','Heading 2'),('The evidence is limited to one intentionally vulnerable AWS Terraform module. The semantic policy crosswalk was prepared by one analyst and needs independent review. No LLM API responses or repeated model runs are present. The GitHub repository corpus and Terraform Registry modules remain uncollected. The study therefore cannot generalise static-tool counts to production repositories or answer its model-performance questions.','Normal'),('6.4 Future Work','Heading 2'),('The first priority is to have a second security reviewer adjudicate the policy crosswalk and record disagreements. The study should then collect the planned GitHub repositories and Registry modules, scan only the defined Terraform scope, and freeze the resulting dataset. If LLM use remains out of scope, the research questions and title should be revised to describe a static-tool comparison rather than attributing results to GPT-4o, Claude, or Gemini.','Normal'),('If the proposed LLM comparison proceeds through another approved route, future work should evaluate zero-shot and few-shot conditions using actual logged outputs, repeat runs to measure response variance, and apply the remediation rubric with blinded reviewers. Further extensions could test chain-of-thought prompting, include Azure and Google Cloud configurations, evaluate newer model releases, and explore integration into continuous integration and continuous delivery pipelines. Any extended evaluation should preserve the distinction between model-generated findings and scanner-generated labels.','Normal'),('The evaluation protocol should also make the unit of analysis explicit for every metric. Multiple policy findings can refer to one underlying weakness, and one resource can contain more than one distinct control failure. A preregistered mapping codebook, blinded adjudication, and sensitivity analysis under strict-ID and semantic-mapping rules would show how much the results depend on the choice of ground truth.','Normal'),('6.5 Closing Statement','Heading 2'),('The TerraGoat scans establish a useful preliminary dataset and show that cross-tool agreement requires semantic mapping at the resource level. They do not yet demonstrate whether LLMs detect Terraform security misconfigurations accurately or recommend reliable fixes. Those conclusions require either actual model outputs under the proposed design or a reframed dissertation focused on the completed static analysis.','Normal')]
